Preamble

The GNU Affero General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other kinds of works, specifically designed to ensure
cooperation with the community in the case of network server software.

The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed
to take away your freedom to share and change the works. By contrast,
our General Public Licenses are intended to guarantee your freedom to
share and change all versions of a work--to make sure it remains free
software for all its users. We, the Free Software Foundation, use the
GNU General Public License for most of our software; the Affero General
Public License is a variant which imposes the additional requirement
that the operator of a network server provide the source code to the
users of that server.

When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not price.
Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you have the
freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for them if
you wish), that you receive source code or can get it if you want it,
that you can change the software or use pieces of it in new free
programs, and that you know you can do these things.

Developers that use the GPL protect your rights with two steps: (1)
assert copyright on the software, and (2) offer you this License giving
you permission to copy, distribute and/or modify the software.

A secondary benefit of defending all users' freedom is that other
licenses require that the authors of the software make the source code
available for inspection and modification, which is essential to the
scientific method. If the software is not available for inspection and
modification, then a scientific process of improvement is impossible.

The Affero General Public License is specifically designed for network
server software to ensure that users of the software over a network have
the same freedoms as users who run the software locally. The precise
terms and conditions for copying, distribution and modification follow.

